  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 135 AM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A reinforcing cold front will move through the \nregion tonight. Ahead of it this afternoon and evening, expect some \ngusty winds and a few light showers.\n\nAnother cold front will move through the region Friday night which \nwill bring additional cold and dry air after Thursday's front. Ahead \nof the fropa, a shortwave trough looks to move through the region \nthis evening. This should allow for a few light showers Friday \nevening/night. The most favored location for any rain looks to be \neast of the US-1 corridor, but not everywhere in this area will \nreceive rain. Precipitation amounts should generally just be a trace \nto a few hundredths. Gusty winds will be possible again this \nafternoon ahead of the front, with northwesterly gusts up to about \n20 mph possible.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A Freeze Warning has been issued for the western \nand northern Piedmont tonight, with a Frost Advisory elsewhere. \n\nThe Freeze Watch previously issued has been upgraded to a Warning \nfor tonight. This will include most of the Piedmont. Lows tonight \nwill dip into the upper 20s to lower 30s in the Warning area. The \nonly exception will be in the urban areas where lows will be a few \ndegrees warmer. Regardless, most areas will see their first 32 \ndegree reading in the Warning area. There will be some lingering \ncirrus in the lee of the mountains for a time tonight. However, most \nmodels suggest that the skies will clear between 03z and 06z leading \nto better potential. In addition, winds are expected to become light \nafter 06z, with good radiational cooling. Therefore, scattered frost \ncan be expected in the rest of the region as lows dip into the mid \n30s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3)... A Freeze Watch has been issued for all counties \nfor Saturday night. \n\nThe lows on Saturday night are expected to dip into the mid 20s to \nlower 30s. Thus, a widespread freeze is expected. Generally clear \nskies and light wind with frost points (dew points) in the 20s will \nlead to a fairly long duration freeze for most areas. With the winds \ngoing to light (less than 6 mph) very good radiational cooling will \nlead to at least scattered frost as well. So, the double whammy of a \nfreeze and frost will definitely end the growing season. \n\nIn addition, expect the cold high pressure to continue to extend \ninto the region into mid-week. This will lead to well below normal \ntemperatures in the 25-32 range at night and generally 50-55 during \nthe day. A gradual moderating trend is expected by Thursday and \nFriday as highs return to the mid to upper 60s with lows in the mid \nto upper 30s by late week.",
